---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site, pillars, compliance } from "../data/site.js";
const fo = pillars.find((p) => p.slug === "family-office");

const title = "Family Office Services | Denver & Metro Detroit | Advisant";
const description =
  "Coordinated planning across tax strategy, wealth management, retirement, estate, and risk for individuals and families whose affairs have outgrown piecemeal advice.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "@id": `${site.domain}/family-office/#service`,
  name: "Family Office Services",
  description,
  provider: { "@id": `${site.domain}/#organization` },
  areaServed: [
    { "@type": "State", name: "Colorado" },
    { "@type": "State", name: "Michigan" },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/family-office/" schema={schema}>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="formline formline--onDark">Family Office Services</p>
          <h1>One plan, holding the whole picture.</h1>
          <p>
            Tax, investments, retirement, estate, and risk are usually handled by different
            people who never speak. Each is competent inside their own lane. Nobody owns the
            seams — and the seams are where the money is.
          </p>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn" href={site.applyUrl}>Schedule a consultation</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/tax-strategy/">Tax strategy</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="credential">
          <div class="credential__seal" aria-hidden="true">EA</div>
          <h2>Not only for families.</h2>
          <p>
            Individuals with concentrated stock, a business interest, or a retirement
            transition in view benefit from the same coordination. Complexity is the
            qualifier, not household size.
          </p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Cadence</dt><dd>Quarterly, plus as needed</dd>
            <dt>Coordination</dt><dd>Attorney &amp; advisors</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="formline">The model</p>
      <h2 style="max-width:26ch">You are at the center. Everyone else works around you.</h2>
      <p class="measure" style="margin-top:1rem">
        The Family Office Advantage&reg; puts you in the middle and builds outward. Closest to
        you sit your Family Office Director, your Family Office Partner, and a Client Success
        Team &mdash; the people you actually speak to. Around them, five disciplines: tax
        strategy, wealth management, risk mitigation, retirement planning, and estate planning.
        Behind those, five specialist teams &mdash; tax and legal, investment, planning and
        analysis, compliance, and client communication.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        The structure exists so that you stop being the person relaying information between
        professionals who have never met. That coordination is the product.
      </p>
      <figure class="wheel">
        <img src="/family-office-advantage.jpg"
             alt="The Family Office Advantage: the client at the center, surrounded by a Family Office Director, Family Office Partner, and Client Success Team, then five disciplines — tax strategy, wealth management, risk mitigation, retirement planning, and estate planning — supported by tax and legal, investment, planning and analysis, compliance, and client communication teams."
             width="980" height="1100" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>
          The Family Office Advantage&reg;, Control the Controllables&reg;, Time Optimized
          Planning&trade;, and Taxes First, Then Math&reg; are marks of Financial Gravity
          Family Office Services, LLC, used with permission.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--sunk">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">Why it matters</p>
        <h2>The problem is fragmentation, not arithmetic</h2>
        <p>
          Most people in this position already have good advisors. An attorney who drafted the
          documents. An investment advisor managing the portfolio. A preparer filing the
          returns. An agent who wrote the policies. Each is competent. None of them owns what
          happens between them.
        </p>
        <p>
          A distribution timed without reference to other income. A charitable gift made in
          cash when appreciated stock was sitting in the account. A portfolio allocated without
          regard to which account holds what. A trust paying tax at compressed rates for years
          because nobody modeled distributing income to beneficiaries. An entity that made
          sense at formation and has been quietly wrong since.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our role is to sit across those seams, hold the consolidated view, bring the other
          advisors into the same conversation, and raise the question that falls between two
          people's remits.
        </p>

        <h2>What is a family office?</h2>
        <p>
          The wealthiest Americans are also the most successful investors, and that is neither
          a secret nor a coincidence. They are served by family offices: a structure that
          integrates tax, wealth, and risk management into one cohesive experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          In ordinary financial services, advice is segregated into very specific areas of
          expertise rather than connected across disciplines. The result is conflicts, higher
          costs, and inefficiency from working with multiple, often competing firms. The same
          integrated approach once reserved for the ultra-wealthy is now available far more
          broadly &mdash; to households, individuals, and business owners.
        </p>

        <h2>What you can expect</h2>

        <h3>Proactive tax planning</h3>
        <p>
          Most financial professionals neglect this discipline. We believe it is not what you
          make but what you keep that matters. Taxes are a drag on portfolio performance,
          wealth accumulation, and lifestyle choices, and we work to lower them in ways that
          are legal, moral, and ethical.
        </p>

        <h3>Control the Controllables&reg;</h3>
        <p>
          The financial services industry spends enormous effort trying to control things that
          cannot be predicted &mdash; market direction, volatility, inflation, interest rates.
          Our approach focuses on what can be controlled: costs, tax efficiency,
          diversification, and maximum loss exposure.
        </p>

        <h3>Multi-generational focus</h3>
        <p>
          Long-term planning is the norm among family offices, with the goal of maximizing
          wealth transfer to succeeding generations or, in many cases, making an impact through
          charitable giving.
        </p>

        <h3>Risk management</h3>
        <p>
          Risks are magnified for the simple reason that you have more to protect. Wealth can
          make you a target, and the complexity of your holdings can create unwanted personal
          liabilities &mdash; exposure that careful planning manages.
        </p>

        <h3>Prudence and discipline</h3>
        <p>
          As a general rule, family offices do not take uncompensated risks. They avoid
          speculation, preferring an institutional approach to asset allocation and portfolio
          management, and accept the ups and downs of markets in exchange for long-term wealth
          building.
        </p>

        <h3>Best interests standard</h3>
        <p>
          Also known as the fiduciary rule. We follow this standard and put the interests and
          welfare of our clients first, searching for and implementing only what we believe are
          the most beneficial solutions for the unique needs of the people we serve.
        </p>

        <h2>Five disciplines, one plan</h2>

        <h3>Tax strategy</h3>
        <p>
          Highly appreciated assets, asset-location optimization across taxable and
          tax-deferred accounts, loss harvesting, and business structuring &mdash; handled with
          the whole picture in view rather than account by account.
        </p>

        <h3>Wealth management</h3>
        <p>
          Control the Controllables&reg;, concentration avoidance, cost containment, and
          risk-aligned asset allocation. Allocation matched to the risk you actually need to
          take rather than the risk you could tolerate.
        </p>

        <h3>Risk mitigation</h3>
        <p>
          Income stabilization, asset protection, longevity security, and the legacy and estate
          planning that sits alongside them. Wealth attracts claims, and complexity creates
          personal liability that most people have never had anyone look at as a whole.
        </p>

        <h3>Retirement planning</h3>
        <p>
          Risk alignment, liquidity alignment, Social Security strategy, and Time Optimized
          Planning&trade;. Distribution sequencing across account types is usually the largest
          single tax variable in retirement and the least examined.
        </p>

        <h3>Estate planning</h3>
        <p>
          Tax mitigation, specialized trusts, family foundations, and coordination with your
          attorney rather than around them. We handle the fiduciary returns and the gift and
          generation-skipping reporting that follow.
        </p>

        <h2>Planning across generations, not tax years</h2>
        <p>
          The organizing question is what transfers, to whom, and in what form. Every annual
          decision either serves that or works against it. That means gifting on a schedule
          rather than in a panic, deliberate use of the annual exclusion and lifetime
          exemption, allocating GST exemption where it does the most work, and funding
          charitable giving with appreciated assets rather than cash.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is not what you make. It is what you keep, and what reaches the next generation
          intact.
        </p>

        <h2>How engagements run</h2>
        <p>
          We start with a structural review: every entity, every trust, the ownership chart,
          the last two years of returns, the current allocation, and a conversation with your
          attorney. That review usually surfaces two or three things worth acting on before it
          is finished.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there the cadence is quarterly, with estimated payments and cash flow managed
          across the whole structure rather than entity by entity, and a planning session
          before year-end while elections are still available.
        </p>

        <h2>What we handle</h2>
        <ul>{fo.points.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">A fit if</p>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li>Your advisors do not talk to each other</li>
          <li>You hold concentrated or highly appreciated stock</li>
          <li>A trust holds an interest in a business</li>
          <li>You file returns for several related entities</li>
          <li>Charitable giving is meaningful and unstructured</li>
          <li>A retirement transition is within a decade</li>
          <li>A generational transfer is in view</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" href={site.applyUrl}>Schedule a consultation</a>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4rem;font-size:.9rem">
          <a href="/fit-check/">Advisor or preparer? Take the fit check &rarr;</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap measure">
      <p class="formline">Where to start</p>
      <h2>Taxes First, Then Math&reg;</h2>
      <p>
        Most plans are built on returns and then taxed as an afterthought. We work the other
        way around, because the tax treatment of a portfolio is knowable in advance and market
        returns are not.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <a class="btn" href={site.applyUrl}>Schedule a consultation</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--sunk">
    <div class="wrap measure">
      <p class="formline">Important disclosures</p>
      <p class="disclosure">{compliance.rlaLine}</p>
      <p class="disclosure" style="margin-top:1rem">{compliance.taxSeparationLine}</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;font-size:.85rem">
        <a href={compliance.advUrl}>Form ADV Part 2A</a> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
        <a href={compliance.crsUrl}>Form CRS</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
